WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.194 --> 00:00:02.741
I am taking this month off,
and while I'm away,

00:00:02.741 --> 00:00:04.437
some other folks are
taking over my channel.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.460
We start this week with a couple of
astronomy and physics majors who,

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.840
along with Professor Chris Impey
and their team at the University of Arizona,

00:00:10.848 --> 00:00:14.052
make a channel called
Active Galactic Videos.

00:00:14.052 --> 00:00:17.593
Now the video they've sent is
a little rough around the edges in places

00:00:17.593 --> 00:00:21.455
but the idea is so good
that I am genuinely annoyed

00:00:21.455 --> 00:00:23.472
that I couldn't go out
and film this myself.

00:00:23.472 --> 00:00:26.291
So Jenny, Melissa, over to you.

00:00:26.291 --> 00:00:27.500
- Hi, I'm Jenny.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.354
- And I'm Melissa, and we're here

00:00:29.354 --> 00:00:32.258
underneath the University of
Arizona's football stadium

00:00:32.258 --> 00:00:34.737
in the Richard F. Caris Mirror Lab.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:37.554
Behind me is the world's
largest spinning furnace

00:00:37.554 --> 00:00:41.124
filled with 38,000 pounds of molten glass.

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:42.727
- Over the next couple of months,

00:00:42.727 --> 00:00:45.699
the glass will slowly cool
and start to form a mirror,

00:00:45.699 --> 00:00:49.144
one of seven that'll be part of
the Giant Magellan Telescope.

00:00:49.144 --> 00:00:53.640
Once completed, the GMT will
be the largest telescope in the world.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.713
The GMT is being built in Chile

00:00:55.713 --> 00:00:59.011
at the Las Campanas Observatory
in the Atacama Desert,

00:00:59.011 --> 00:01:02.086
and it's scheduled to
see first light in 2022.

00:01:02.086 --> 00:01:06.679
This massive telescope will have ten times
the resolution of the Hubble Space Telescope,

00:01:06.679 --> 00:01:08.966
and more than seven times
the light-gathering power

00:01:08.966 --> 00:01:10.973
of the largest telescopes today.

00:01:10.973 --> 00:01:13.933
The GMT is made of seven
eight-and-a-half-meter mirrors

00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:16.498
for a total diameter of 25 meters,

00:01:16.498 --> 00:01:20.486
and it will be used to study
galaxies, stars, and exoplanets.

00:01:20.486 --> 00:01:23.167
- The GMT-5, which is the
mirror being cast right now

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:25.943
is actually the 20th mirror
made at this facility.

00:01:25.943 --> 00:01:27.709
Other mirrors have been
made for other telescopes

00:01:27.709 --> 00:01:30.895
like the MMT, the Large
Synoptic Survey Telescope,

00:01:30.895 --> 00:01:34.214
the Twin Magellan telescopes,
and the Large Binocular Telescope.

00:01:34.214 --> 00:01:37.720
It was built under the stadium
to take advantage of the large columns,

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.533
which act as load-bearing structures
for all of the heavy equipment needed.

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:44.454
It's also nice and quiet when
there aren't football games going on.

00:01:44.454 --> 00:01:48.212
It's a great way to make football
benefit both the university and science.

00:01:48.212 --> 00:01:52.871
- To start off, we need
38,540 pounds of glass.

00:01:52.871 --> 00:01:54.867
Sand is shipped from all over the world

00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:57.899
and brought to Japan, to
the Ohara glass factory.

00:01:57.899 --> 00:02:02.217
They're the only factory in the world 
that can make the E6 low-expansion glass

00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:04.224
that's needed for the GMT mirrors.

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:06.380
The glass is made in 2,000 pound batches

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.781
made exclusively for the
University of Arizona.

00:02:09.781 --> 00:02:13.440
It takes around two and a half years
to make enough glass for a single mirror,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.740
so, you know, plan ahead.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.680
- The glass is then
shipped here to Tucson.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.738
Once the mirror lab gets it,
they have to
clean it, inspect it, and weigh it.

00:02:21.738 --> 00:02:26.040
If there are any stresses or imperfections
in the glass, then the piece is rejected

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.212
and made into paperweights.

00:02:29.212 --> 00:02:34.052
- Each individual piece of glass is placed
carefully into the mold and inside the oven.

00:02:34.052 --> 00:02:36.896
The 40-foot diameter oven
is then gradually heated

00:02:36.896 --> 00:02:40.504
to a temperature of
1,350 degrees Fahrenheit

00:02:40.504 --> 00:02:42.255
over the course of a week.

00:02:42.255 --> 00:02:44.007
There are cameras in the
oven to monitor the process

00:02:44.007 --> 00:02:47.168
but once it begins,
there's no turning back.

00:02:47.168 --> 00:02:50.963
At this point, the massive oven
is filled with 19 tonnes of glass

00:02:50.963 --> 00:02:54.254
and starts to spin five
revolutions per minute.

00:02:54.254 --> 00:02:57.835
Once the oven reaches
2,129 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:02:57.835 --> 00:03:02.494
or 1,165 degrees Celsius,
the consistency of the glass

00:03:02.494 --> 00:03:06.004
is about the same as honey and
starts to flow into the mold.

00:03:06.004 --> 00:03:09.140
- And it might make sense
why we need an oven to melt the glass,

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.222
but you might be wondering, 
"why does it spin?"

00:03:12.222 --> 00:03:14.216
- It spins to make the shape
we need for a mirror,

00:03:14.216 --> 00:03:15.539
a parabola.

00:03:15.539 --> 00:03:19.995
Modern telescopes use parabolic mirrors
to collect and concentrate light.

00:03:19.995 --> 00:03:23.060
They use the parabolic shape to
get the light into a central focus point,

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.057
and the bigger the mirror,
the more light is collected.

00:03:26.057 --> 00:03:29.241
- But what does have to do with spinning
besides making us dizzy?

00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:31.241
Well, let's take a look.

00:03:32.223 --> 00:03:35.347
So it turns out that spinning
a liquid actually causes it

00:03:35.347 --> 00:03:37.977
to automatically become
this parabolic shape.

00:03:37.977 --> 00:03:39.527
So whenever you spin the glass,

00:03:39.527 --> 00:03:41.849
it creates the perfect
shape needed for a mirror.

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:44.591
This is a massive improvement
over how mirrors used to be made

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:47.385
and it "grinds" years off the process.

00:03:47.385 --> 00:03:51.292
The mirror spends three months
cooling and annealing in the oven.

00:03:51.292 --> 00:03:54.852
So now it's the right shape
but it isn't quite done yet.

00:03:54.852 --> 00:03:57.657
When light hits the mirror,
the surface has to be smoother

00:03:57.657 --> 00:04:00.211
than a fraction of the
wavelength of light.

00:04:00.211 --> 00:04:02.344
Otherwise the image is distorted.

00:04:02.344 --> 00:04:03.941
In order to get it this smooth,

00:04:03.941 --> 00:04:07.094
the mirror lab spends two
years polishing the glass.

00:04:07.094 --> 00:04:10.218
It has to be smooth to the point
where no imperfection on the surface

00:04:10.218 --> 00:04:12.196
is larger than a virus.

00:04:12.196 --> 00:04:18.137
After all of that, after melting the glass
in this giant oven, and polishing it for two years,

00:04:18.137 --> 00:04:21.976
all you have is a huge,
really smooth piece of glass.

00:04:21.976 --> 00:04:25.760
- So the mirror lab actually
doesn't make any mirrors.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.842
- The final step in turning
this giant piece of glass

00:04:27.842 --> 00:04:31.587
into a mirror is actually done
on-site at the observatory.

00:04:31.587 --> 00:04:34.194
A layer of aluminum
about 100 nanometers thick

00:04:34.194 --> 00:04:36.398
is deposited onto the surface.

00:04:36.398 --> 00:04:38.052
To cover a mirror of this size,

00:04:38.052 --> 00:04:41.200
it actually only takes a
soda can's worth of metal.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.699
- Unlike your mirrors at
home, the reflective coating

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:46.662
is only on the front surface,
so it's very delicate

00:04:46.662 --> 00:04:49.776
and needs to be re-aluminized
every so many years.

00:04:49.776 --> 00:04:52.277
- Thank you to everyone here
at the Richard F. Caris Mirror Lab

00:04:52.277 --> 00:04:53.761
and the Steward Observatory.

00:04:53.761 --> 00:04:56.172
Tours are open to the public
every day of the week.

00:04:56.172 --> 00:04:58.976
- Go subscribe to Active Galactic
Videos for more like that.

00:04:58.976 --> 00:05:00.464
I would recommend starting with the video

00:05:00.464 --> 00:05:03.253
where they get to walk on
the dish of the telescope

00:05:03.253 --> 00:05:07.016
and yes, I am extremely envious
that they got to do that.

00:05:07.016 --> 00:05:11.843
Next week, a language video that you
are probably going to need subtitles for.

